==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fro|ditie}} or {{m|fro|dité}}, from {{m|fro|ditier}}, from {{der|en|la|dictāre}} (participle {{m|en|dictatus}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪti/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-ditty.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|En-uk-ditty.ogg|Audio (UK)}}
* Homophones: [[diddy]] (US)
* {{rhymes|en|ɪti}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|ditties}}

# A short [[verse]] or [[tune]].
#: {{ux|en|The Acme mattress '''ditty''' has been stuck in my head all day.}}
#* {{rfdatek|en|John Milton}}
#*: religious, martial, or civil ditties
#* {{rfdatek|en|Sandys}}
#*: And to the warbling lute soft '''ditties''' sing.
# A [[saying]] or [[utterance]], especially one that is short and frequently repeated.
#* {{rfdatek|en|Edmund Spenser}}
#*: O, too high '''ditty''' for my simple rhyme.

